course: dictionary
lesson: 8198
title: When a word hides the truth
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words are chosen to make bad news sound better. A softer word used in place of a harsh one is called a euphemism. Restructuring is one of these words. Its everyday meaning is changing how something is organised, like rearranging a school timetable.
explain: In business, «restructuring» often means a company is cutting jobs. Look at the context: «A company that sacks hundreds of workers may call it "downsizing", "rightsizing" or "restructuring", which is spin to make it sound sensible.» The clue is sacks hundreds of workers: the big, calm word covers up losing jobs.
explain: To check a word like this, ask two questions. What is the sentence really saying? Who is saying it, and why? Workers who hear "restructuring" often feel worried, and that is exactly the feeling the word can hide.
fact: Restructuring means changing how something is built or organised.
fact: In business, «restructuring» often means reorganising a company in a way that usually means cutting jobs.
fact: A euphemism is a gentler word used to avoid a harsh one.
fact: «Downsizing» and «rightsizing» are other words companies use when they cut staff.
fact: «Restructure» is the verb, so a company can restructure, and the act of doing it is restructuring.
exercise: «The school restructured its timetable so that Year 5 had music on Monday.» What does restructured mean here? | *Changed how the timetable was organised | Painted the timetable a new colour | Cut the timetable in half | Lost the timetable for a week
exercise: «The company announced a restructuring, and two hundred jobs will be lost.» What does the news really tell you? | Nobody has to change anything | *Jobs are being cut | Everyone is getting a pay rise | The company is moving to a new building
exercise: Why might a company say "restructuring" instead of "sacking workers"? | It means exactly the same as sacking, so it is more honest | It shows the company is very happy | *It sounds calmer and more sensible, which hides the job losses | It is a shorter word for sacking
test: Mia reads: «Restructuring means reorganising a company, usually with job cuts.» What does the word usually suggest in a business story? | *Workers might lose their jobs | Nobody will notice any change | The company is giving out free holidays | The office is being painted
test: What does euphemism mean? | A word that means the same as sacking | A word from another country | *A softer word used in place of a harder one | A very loud word
exam: Why should you read the context before trusting a big word in the news? | Because all long words are always true | *Because a word like restructuring can hide bad news and make it sound calm | Because context only matters in stories | Because dictionaries never explain business words
